-- help-inbox.stdout --
Usage: homebot [--all]

Lists what needs you, most urgent first, each with the command that fixes it,
and what the background helper did since you last looked. `homebot inbox` is
the same command.

Options:
  --all        show every item, not only the first five
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-inbox.stderr --
-- help-next.stdout --
Usage: homebot next

Runs the fix for the first inbox item, the command shown next to it. When the
fix is a shell command homebot shouldn't run for you, it prints it instead.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-next.stderr --
-- help-unpack.stdout --
Usage: homebot unpack [profile]

Runs a profile's steps in order, without asking first: package installs
(brew, apt, flatpak...), links, secrets and `run:` commands. Each step is
printed as it starts. Without a name it uses this machine's profile from
homebot.yml (profiles.hosts, else profiles.default).

Preview the steps with --dry-run. A step that fails stops the run and prints
the --from that resumes after you fix it. Steps that are already done (a
package that is installed, a link that exists) do nothing the second time.

Options:
  --dry-run    list the steps without running any
  --from N     start at step N, skipping the ones before
  -h, --help   this page

Examples:
  homebot unpack --dry-run         what this machine's profile would do
  homebot unpack server --from 4   resume the server profile at step 4
-- help-unpack.stderr --
-- help-pack.stdout --
Usage: homebot pack <path>...

Moves a file or folder from $HOME into the repo and puts a link in its place,
so it is tracked from then on. It goes to autolink/common unless --layer says
otherwise. With --private it is sealed under private/ (encrypted with age)
instead. Commit it with homebot save.

Options:
  --layer common|linux|macos   the layer to put it in (default: common)
  --private                    a secret: seal it under private/
  -h, --help                   this page

Examples:
  homebot pack ~/.config/zed                 track Zed's settings on every machine
  homebot pack --layer linux ~/.config/foo   track it on Linux only
  homebot pack --private ~/.ssh/id_ed25519   track a key, encrypted
-- help-pack.stderr --
-- help-link.stdout --
Usage: homebot link

Links everything under autolink/ (and unlocked secrets under private/) into
$HOME. It never overwrites anything: what is in the way is listed as blocked.
A file identical to the repo's copy is replaced by the link, since nothing is
lost.

--backup moves the blockers aside first, renamed <name>.homebot-backup-<time>,
and then links. homebot cleanup goes through those backups later.

Options:
  --dry-run    show what would change, change nothing
  --backup     move what is in the way aside, then link
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-link.stderr --
-- help-unlink.stdout --
Usage: homebot unlink

Removes every link homebot made. The files stay in the repo, so the links
come back with the next homebot link, and within the hour from the background
helper (homebot tidy). To keep a file unlinked, take it out of the repo.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-unlink.stderr --
-- help-cleanup.stdout --
Usage: homebot cleanup

Goes through the backups homebot link --backup left
(<name>.homebot-backup-<time>) one at a time. Each shows how it differs from
what is linked there now (beside the choices, when fzf is installed), and you
pick:

  delete   remove the backup; what is linked there now stays
  keep     leave it; homebot asks again next time
  use      copy the backup over the repo file, then remove it
  merge    take some of the backup's lines into the repo file

merge opens lazygit when it is installed. The backup's lines are the green
ones: space takes the line under the cursor, shift+arrows select several, d
drops them, q finishes. Without lazygit it is git add -p: y takes a block, n
skips it, s splits it smaller, e edits it. What you took is written to the
repo file, and the same backup comes back with what is left, so you can
delete it.

Options:
  --list       only list the backups
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-cleanup.stderr --
-- help-keys.stdout --
Usage: homebot keys init

Creates the age key pair that seals private/. It asks for a passphrase, then
writes private/identity.age (the key, encrypted with that passphrase) and
private/recipients.txt (the public key). Run it once ever; other machines get
the key with the repo and run homebot unlock.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-keys.stderr --
-- help-unlock.stdout --
Usage: homebot unlock

Decrypts the secrets in private/ and links them into $HOME. A secret that
changed both here and in the repo is left alone and listed as a conflict.

Options:
  --theirs     for conflicts, take the repo's version
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-unlock.stderr --
-- help-lock.stdout --
Usage: homebot lock

Seals the secrets in private/ that changed since they were last sealed. The
background helper does this on its own before each autosave.

Options:
  --ours       for conflicts, keep this machine's version
  --wipe       also delete the decrypted copies (keeps any that aren't safely sealed)
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-lock.stderr --
-- help-triage.stdout --
Usage: homebot triage

Asks once about each new thing in the watched folders (~/.config/*, ~/.*) and
each untracked file in the repo. Each answer is saved right away:

  t        track it everywhere (moves it into autolink/common, links it back)
  o        track it on this OS only
  i        ignore it for good (written to decisions.yml)
  s        snooze it for 7 days (this machine only)
  enter    next
  q        stop

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-triage.stderr --
-- help-inventory.stdout --
Usage: homebot inventory

One screen: this machine's profile and whether it changed since the last
unpack, links, secrets, things to triage, the repo and its remote, package
exports and the background helper.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-inventory.stderr --
-- help-remote.stdout --
Usage: homebot remote [url]

Without a remote, offers to create a private repository with gh (GitHub) or
glab (GitLab), when one is installed and logged in, or takes a URL you paste.
Then it pushes. With a URL, makes that the remote (origin).

With a remote already set, checks that it can be reached. When an https
remote fails for lack of a login and you have an ssh key, it offers the ssh
address of the same repository.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page

Examples:
  homebot remote                               check the remote, or set one up
  homebot remote git@github.com:you/home.git   use this one
-- help-remote.stderr --
-- help-save.stdout --
Usage: homebot save

Seals changed secrets, commits the changes to tracked files and package
snapshots, then pulls (rebasing local commits) and pushes. New files aren't
committed: triage decides about them first. The background helper does the
same on its own (autosave in homebot.yml).

Options:
  -m, --message TEXT   the commit message (default: one listing the files)
  --no-push            commit and pull, but don't push
  -h, --help           this page
-- help-save.stderr --
-- help-export.stdout --
Usage: homebot export [name...]

Writes what is installed (Brewfile, apt and flatpak lists, vendor apt
repositories...) into manifests/, as configured under exports in homebot.yml.
Without names it runs every export for this OS. The background helper runs
them about once a day.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page

Examples:
  homebot export apt apt-repos   after installing a package from a new apt repository
-- help-export.stderr --
-- help-tidy.stdout --
Usage: homebot tidy

Runs what the background helper runs on its timer: installs the commit guard
if it's missing, seals secrets, refreshes package exports that are due,
autosaves and syncs (when homebot.yml turns autosave on), fixes links that
are safe to fix, and refreshes the inbox.

Options:
  --notify     show a desktop notification when something needs you
  --quiet      print nothing
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-tidy.stderr --
-- help-setup.stdout --
Usage: homebot setup

Takes you from nothing to an empty inbox, one question at a time. It checks
for git and gitleaks, clones your home repo or starts a new one in ~/.home,
installs the commit guard and the background helper (a systemd user timer on
Linux, a launchd agent on macOS), and offers to load the shell integration,
back the repo up and commit. Steps already done are skipped, so running it
again is safe. Without a terminal it asks nothing, creates no repo, and says
what is left.

Options:
  --remove       uninstall the background helper
  --name WORD    choose a command name for your homebot
  --persona ID   choose the persona for this home repo
  -h, --help     this page
-- help-setup.stderr --
-- help-actions.stdout --
Usage: homebot actions

Lists the actions profile steps can use: the built-in ones (link, unlock,
run) and the scripts in homebot/actions/.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-actions.stderr --
-- help-action.stdout --
Usage: homebot action <name> [args...]

Runs one action with the environment a profile step gets. Everything after
the name goes to the action, so `homebot action <name> --help` reaches the
action; this page is `homebot action --help`.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page

Examples:
  homebot action apt-repos linux/apt-repos   add the vendor apt repositories
-- help-action.stderr --
-- help-shell.stdout --
Usage: homebot shell zsh

Prints the zsh integration: the prompt line that says when something needs
you, refreshed in the background. Load it from .zshrc:

  eval "$(homebot shell zsh)"

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-shell.stderr --
-- help-name.stdout --
Usage: homebot name [word]

Shows the name in homebot.yml and the name that works here. Give a word
to make it a command on this machine, or use --off to remove a link homebot
made. A name may need a new shell before it works.

Options:
  --off        remove the name and a link homebot made
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-name.stderr --
-- help-refresh.stdout --
Usage: homebot refresh

Recomputes the inbox cache the shell prompt reads. Most commands do this on
their own when they finish.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-refresh.stderr --
-- help-hook.stdout --
Usage: homebot hook pre-commit

The check git runs before each commit in the repo (installed by homebot
setup). It refuses plaintext under private/ and anything gitleaks flags.

Options:
  -h, --help   this page
-- help-hook.stderr --
